# ── THIRD SEEDING — the STEADY-STATE case, and the reason it is worth naming separately ──────────
# Actions run 31369960469, the first run on TRUNK (sha 018381c6). 345 of 345 ran; 338 green, 6 red,
# 1 cut. None of these 7 appeared in the two branch-tree runs above: trunk had simply moved on, and
# every suite it gained landed INSIDE the partition by default, exactly as designed.
#
# This is the maintenance loop, and it is the honest ongoing cost of defaulting new suites IN: a
# machine-coupled suite reds the producer on the land that adds it, until one line appears here.
# The alternative — defaulting new suites OUT — costs the thing that cannot be recovered, because a
# suite silently absent from the partition looks exactly like a suite that passed. The job summary
# now prints a paste-ready block for precisely this, so curing it is a copy rather than a transcription.
# run 31369960469: notok=3
tests/account-fact-derivation.bats
# run 31369960469: notok=1, 72s
tests/compressor-sentinel.bats
# run 31369960469: notok=1
tests/pool-floor.bats
# run 31369960469: notok=1
tests/rotate-autonomy-logs.bats
# run 31369960469: notok=1
tests/session-end-gc-lock.bats
# run 31369960469: notok=1
tests/tsv-field-collapse.bats
# run 31369960469: CUT at the 120s bound — a non-verdict, not a red. Excluded on COST, like
# postland-verify.bats below, and re-admissible if it ever gets faster.
tests/worktree-gc-infra.bats

# ── FOURTH SEEDING — a THIRD exclusion class: hostile to the runner itself ───────────────────────
# Actions run 31371884433 (trunk 77656a65). Shard 6 died with **exit 143 — SIGTERM** immediately
# after tests/pkill-scope.bats reported GREEN, taking the 34 suites behind it with it and folding
# the whole run to a short 304-of-338. The suite passed its own assertions and then killed the
# process running it.
#
# That is neither of the two classes above. It is not machine-coupled (it passes) and it is not too
# slow (15s). It is HOSTILE TO A SHARDED RUNNER: this repo's own land docs record that
# `pkill -f bats…` SIGKILLs every concurrent gate on the box because every bats command line
# contains the same string, and a CI runner is just another box where that is true. A suite whose
# subject is process-killing cannot share a process tree with a runner that has more work queued.
# The per-suite `timeout` gives each suite its own process group and does NOT help here — a pattern
# match on a command line crosses process groups by design, which is the whole point of the tool
# under test.
# run 31371884433: shard SIGTERM (143) immediately after this suite, 34 suites lost
tests/pkill-scope.bats
# run 31371884433: notok=1
tests/live-session-registry-atomic.bats

# ── the land and verify machinery itself ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are the subjects that OWN the on-box verdict, so excluding them from the off-box second
# opinion costs the least of anything here — the on-box corpus is where their claim belongs.
# run 31362043861: notok=40
tests/ship-land.bats
# run 31362043861: notok=10
tests/postland-verify-bisect-bound.bats
# run 31362043861: CUT at the 120s per-suite bound (its own header records ~50 min solo on-box).
# NOT a red — it named zero failing tests. It is here because a suite measured in TENS OF MINUTES
# cannot be part of a per-commit second opinion, which is a statement about cost, not correctness.
tests/postland-verify.bats
